-- Migration 50: reconcile pre-Track-B entity schema drift (Q-B-1)
--
-- Migration 39 (re)defined `entity` for Track B (canonical_name + a free-string
-- entity_type), but SurrealDB's DEFINE TABLE/FIELD does NOT remove pre-existing
-- field definitions. On any database that carried a pre-39 `entity` table, the
-- legacy `name` (required), `hash_id` (required) fields and the `entity_type`
-- enum ASSERT survived alongside the new fields and are still enforced. The
-- Track B write-path writes `canonical_name` (not `name`/`hash_id`), so every
-- live upsert fails with "Found NONE for field `name`/`hash_id`" or an
-- entity_type enum violation — while fresh databases (which only ever saw
-- migration 39, e.g. the testcontainers) pass. This is the documented Q-B-1
-- drift surfacing.
--
-- Reconcile by RELAXING the legacy constraints so the Track B write-path
-- (canonical_name) succeeds. The persistence service dual-writes
-- name=canonical_name on create, so new entities populate both the new
-- (canonical_name) and the read-path (name) fields.
--
-- We deliberately do NOT backfill the pre-existing legacy rows here: those rows
-- predate this schema and violate other current field constraints (e.g.
-- avg_confidence NONE vs required float), so any UPDATE to them re-validates the
-- whole record and fails. They already carry a populated `name` and render
-- correctly; a separate purge/normalize of the legacy noise is tracked
-- independently. New writes are unaffected.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE name ON entity TYPE option<string>;
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE hash_id ON entity TYPE option<string>;
-- Drop the legacy enum ASSERT (the extraction service normalizes types).
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE entity_type ON entity TYPE string;
